# lib/pkgmap/windows.map — logical package name -> the ONE provider that
# installs it on this machine.
#
# This file sits beside apt.map and pacman.map and is read by the same
# resolver, in the same format, with the same @facet ranking: `windows` is
# what OSR_PKG holds here, exactly as `apt` is what it holds on a Debian box.
# What differs is only which providers a row may name, because there is no one
# native manager on this system to be the default -- which of the three serves
# a package is precisely what a row exists to say.
#
# Format:  <name>[@facet] = <provider>
#
#   provider := scoop:<id>  | choco:<id> | winget:<id>
#             | source:<builder>          a builder in lib/build.c
#             | script:<url>              the vendor's own install script
#
# THIS MAP DOES NOT FALL THROUGH TO any.map, and that is the one deliberate
# difference from every other map here. any.map holds the rows that are the
# same whichever LINUX package manager is in play -- a source: builder that
# compiles for POSIX, a vendor script that pipes into sh. Reaching one of those
# from Windows would not be a shared answer, it would be the wrong answer, and
# the whole point of a map is that a package's source is decided in advance
# rather than fallen into. A name with no row here is a gap to fix.
#
# ONE PROVIDER PER ROW. This is the rule the whole file exists to enforce, not
# a stylistic preference. scoop, choco and winget are independent namespaces:
# a name that is the real project in one of them can be an unrelated upload by
# anyone in the next, because the manager a project does not ship to is
# exactly where its name is still free to claim. A row that listed several
# providers would mean "try them until one works", which turns "the intended
# source is missing on this machine" into "install whatever the next namespace
# has under this name" — a different publisher's software, silently. So a row
# names one provider, osr uses that provider, and a row that names two is a
# map error (OSR_WINPKG_BAD_ROW), not a chain. Same rule, same reason, and the
# same provider vocabulary as lib/pkgmap/*.map on the Linux side.
#
# A MISSING MANAGER IS INSTALLED, NOT ROUTED AROUND. If a row's manager is not
# on this machine, osr installs it from its own vendor installer: scoop from
# get.scoop.sh (per-user, no elevation), choco from
# community.chocolatey.org/install.ps1, winget from asheroto/winget-install.
# The last two need Administrator, so the run elevates once, up front, and
# continues elevated — the Windows equivalent of install.sh's `sudo -v`
# warm-up. That is why this file needs no fallbacks: the provider a row names
# can always be made to exist.
#
# WHERE A PACKAGE MUST COME FROM SOMEWHERE ELSE ON SOME MACHINES, SAY SO WITH
# AN @facet ROW. Facet qualifiers work exactly as lib/pkg.sh resolves them
# (DESIGN §1a), most specific first:
#
#   name@<release>   Windows DisplayVersion, e.g. 24H2, 23H2  (~ OSR_CODENAME)
#   name@<version>   Windows product major, 11 or 10          (~ OSR_VERSION_ID)
#   name@<arch>      x86_64 | arm64 | x86                     (~ OSR_ARCH)
#   name             unqualified fallback
#
# A qualified row REPLACES the bare row; it does not merge with it. That is
# how a package changes provider per machine — deliberately, in advance, and
# visible in one place — instead of by a runtime scramble. See wezterm below
# for the worked example.
#
# The facets come from `osr detect`, out of the registry and a couple of system
# calls, and they carry the SAME NAMES the Linux side uses -- which is what
# lets one resolver read both maps. OSR_ARCH is x86_64 | arm64 | x86 on either
# system.
#
# THE source: PROVIDER names a builder in lib/build.c's registry -- the same
# registry, and often the same builder, that `source:provide_wezterm` in
# any.map names. A URL can only ever say "download this"; a builder can resolve
# a version, pick an asset per architecture, install its own build dependencies
# through this very map, clone with submodules, run a compiler, and place
# several binaries at the end. Reach for it whenever the install is more than a
# fetch — and note the cost, because it is why managers stay preferred: a
# manager can upgrade and remove what it installed, while a builder's output is
# only ever overwritten in place.
#
# THE script: PROVIDER runs a vendor's own install script (`irm <url> | iex`),
# the route a project means by "paste this line into a shell". Per-user: a
# script that needs Administrator belongs behind a source: builder that can
# say so, so that the run elevates once up front instead of failing mid-script.
#
# WHICH MANAGER A ROW NAMES is decided by the project's own Windows install
# page, not by preference — the manager upstream ships to is the one whose id
# upstream actually controls. Where upstream ranks its options, the first one
# wins; where it lists several without ranking, the tie goes to the
# publisher-qualified id (`Starship.Starship` can only be claimed by starship;
# a bare `starship` can be claimed by anyone). Every row cites the page it came
# from — re-check that page before changing a row.
#
# A scoop id may carry its bucket as `scoop:extras/wezterm`; osr runs
# `scoop bucket add extras` before installing. A bare scoop id must therefore
# be in the `main` bucket, which scoop installs by default.

# Microsoft, "Install PowerShell 7 on Windows": WinGet is listed as the
# "Recommended way to install PowerShell on Windows clients", and the doc's
# "Supported installation methods" section covers only the methods it lists —
# scoop and choco are neither listed nor supported by Microsoft.
pwsh       = winget:Microsoft.PowerShell

# wezterm — the worked example of a provider that changes per architecture.
#
# wezterm.org/install/windows.html lists winget first, then scoop (extras
# bucket), then choco; its scoop route would be scoop:extras/wezterm. But every
# one of those ships x64 ONLY — checked against wez.wezterm's winget installer
# manifest, which declares a single x64 installer — and upstream publishes no
# Windows arm64 release asset at all, so there is no artifact for arm64 to
# download from anywhere. What is left is what upstream documents for exactly
# this case: build it (wezterm.org/install/source.html). provide_wezterm in
# lib/build.c does that, and installs its own git/rust/perl prerequisites
# through this map on the way. It is a long compile, which is precisely why
# only arm64 pays for it -- the POSIX maps reach the same builder name for
# every target, because there no manager ships wezterm at all.
#
# No bare `wezterm` row: upstream has no 32-bit build either, so an x86 machine
# should get "no windows.map row for wezterm", not a silent wrong answer.
wezterm@x86_64 = winget:wez.wezterm
wezterm@arm64  = source:provide_wezterm

# ohmyposh.dev/docs/installation/windows leads with winget and cautions that
# "The chocolatey package is maintained by the community and might not be up to
# date". Not carried in scoop's main or extras bucket at all.
oh-my-posh = winget:JanDeDobbeleer.OhMyPosh

# starship's README lists choco, scoop and winget side by side without ranking
# them, so the tie goes to the publisher-qualified id (see header).
starship   = winget:Starship.Starship

# fastfetch's README lists scoop first, and fastfetch is in scoop's *main*
# bucket (ScoopInstaller/Main), which scoop adds on install — no bucket prefix
# needed. This is the one row that is not winget: upstream ranks scoop first.
fastfetch  = scoop:fastfetch

# --- build dependencies ------------------------------------------------------
# Not rices in themselves: these exist so a builder can install what it needs
# through the map (osr_pkg_need) instead of hardcoding ids, the same way the
# POSIX builders open with `pkg_install build git`. All three are what
# provide_wezterm requires per wezterm.org/install/source.html.

git            = winget:Git.Git
rustup         = winget:Rustlang.Rustup
strawberryperl = winget:StrawberryPerl.StrawberryPerl
